  "full_response": "Creating an exhaustive guide to Linux system administration involves detailed knowledge across various areas. Below is a comprehensive outline to help navigate the primary aspects of Linux system administration. For a full understanding, provisioning for hands-on practice and additional reading from authoritative books and official documentation is recommended.\n\n### 1. Users and Permissions\n\n- **Understanding Users and Groups:**\n  - `/etc/passwd`: Lists user accounts.\n  - `/etc/group`: Defines groups.\n  - `useradd`, `usermod`, `userdel`: Commands to manage users.\n  - `groupadd`, `groupmod`, `groupdel`: Commands to manage groups.\n\n- **File Permissions:**\n  - Read (r), Write (w), Execute (x) permissions.\n  - `chmod`: Modify file mode bits.\n  - `chown`: Change file owner and group.\n  - `umask`: Set default permission for newly created files.\n\n- **Access Control Lists (ACLs):**\n  - Using `getfacl` and `setfacl` for granular permissions.\n  - Enabling/disabling the `acl` filesystem option (`mount` and `tune2fs`).\n\n### 2. Networking\n\n- **Networking Basics:**\n  - `/etc/network/interfaces` and `NetworkManager` for network configuration.\n  - IP addressing (`ifconfig`, `ip`), subnetting, and routing.\n\n- **Configuring Network Services:**\n  - Configuring network interfaces (static IP, DHCP).\n  - DNS configuration (BIND, `/etc/resolv.conf`).\n  - Setting up a firewall (`iptables`, `firewalld`, `ufw`).\n\n- **Remote Access and Management:**\n  - SSH (installation, configuration, key-based authentication).\n  - `scp` and `rsync` for secure file transfer.\n  - Port forwarding and tunneling.\n\n- **Network Services and Protocols:**\n  - NFS, Samba (CIFS/SMB), FTP.\n  - DNS, DHCP, and DNSSEC configuration.\n  - VPN setup (OpenVPN, WireGuard).\n\n### 3. Storage\n\n- **Disk Management:**\n  - Tools: `fdisk`, `parted`, `gparted`.\n  - Filesystems: `ext4`, `xfs`, `btrfs`.\n  - Mounting and Unmounting: `mount`, `umount`.\n\n- **Logical Volume Management (LVM):**\n  - Creating and managing logical volumes with `lvm2`.\n\n- **RA",
